+# Filtering the Veterinarian Directory by Specialty Is In Scope; Free-Text Veterinarian Search Stays Out
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declared search for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian out of scope, leaving only owners searchable. Its rationale was that owner search already demonstrates paged prefix search once, and repeating it for other entities would add surface without teaching anything new about the stack.
+
+A request has since asked to narrow the veterinarian arm of that row: let a reader restrict the veterinarian directory to the veterinarians holding a named specialty. Specialty is an attribute the directory already displays for every veterinarian, so the narrowing exposes existing data through the page address rather than adding a new search capability. The owner has decided the narrowing (2026-08-26).
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Leave NG-9 whole.** Rejected: the row conflates two different things. Free-text search over veterinarian records is genuine new surface; restricting an already-shown attribute to one of its displayed values is not. Keeping them fused would decline a capability the owner wants for a reason that only applies to the other half.
+2. **Drop NG-9 entirely and open veterinarian search.** Rejected: free-text search for a veterinarian still adds surface without teaching a new pattern, exactly as the original rationale holds. Only the attribute-filter half is wanted.
+3. **Narrow NG-9 to admit attribute filtering of an already-shown field** (chosen).
+
+## Decision
+
+NG-9 narrows. Free-text search for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian stays out of scope. Filtering the veterinarian directory by a specialty it already displays is now in scope, recorded as REQ-VET-003. Attribute filtering of a displayed field is not search: it selects among values the page already shows rather than matching free text against records.
+
+This narrowing is a recorded owner decision, per the Non-Goals table's convention that narrowing a row later carries its own non-goal ADR.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- NG-9 keeps free-text search for pets, visits, and veterinarians out; it no longer covers filtering the veterinarian directory by specialty.
+- REQ-VET-003 records the in-scope capability, spanning both the veterinarian directory page and the machine-readable veterinarian list.
+- A visible on-page control for the filter is not part of this decision; the filter is reached through the page address. A control may follow as a separate request.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+- [PRD Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the narrowed NG-9 row.
+- [PRD REQ-VET-003](../prd.md#req-vet-003) — the in-scope specialty filter.
